  <h4 id="prose-links-default">Setup <a class="bx--prose__heading-anchor" href="#prose-links-default" aria-label="Link to Setup">#</a></h4>
  <p>Read the <a href="#links">setup guide</a>, then the <a href="#links">API reference</a>.</p>
</Prose>

### Muted

<Prose linkTone="muted">
  <h4 id="prose-links-muted">Setup <a class="bx--prose__heading-anchor" href="#prose-links-muted" aria-label="Link to Setup">#</a></h4>
  <p>Read the <a href="#links">setup guide</a>, then the <a href="#links">API reference</a>.</p>
</Prose>

## Keyboard keys

Keys look like flat keycaps by default. Set `kbd="muted"` for the shortcut style `Menu` uses, with no box, for dense copy and long key sequences. Either way, keys never change the space between lines.

### Keycap

<Prose>
  <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>P</kbd> to open the palette, then <kbd>Enter</kbd> to run the highlighted command.</p>
</Prose>

### Muted

<Prose kbd="muted">
  <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>P</kbd> to open the palette, then <kbd>Enter</kbd> to run the highlighted command.</p>
</Prose>

## Tables

The first and last columns line up with the copy. By default, `tableWidth="fit"` packs columns at their content width with a gap between them, so a short table reads with the copy around it. Set `tableWidth="full"` for wide or data-heavy tables: the table spans the line length with padded cells.

### Fit

<Prose>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>30 seconds</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>retries</code></td><td>3</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Prose>

### Full

<Prose tableWidth="full">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>30 seconds</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>retries</code></td><td>3</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Prose>

## Section dividers

Set `dividers` to draw a rule above each `h2`, so each section reads as one group even when examples interrupt the copy. An `h2` that opens the Prose gets no rule.

<Prose dividers>
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>Add the package with your package manager.</p>
  <h2>Configure</h2>
  <p>Import the styles once, in your app's entry point.</p>
</Prose>

## Flow container

A page that alternates copy with live examples, such as a Markdown page rendered with one Prose per run of copy, needs space between those runs. Wrap them in an element with the `bx--prose-flow` class: siblings get the flow gap, copy after an example gets more room, and a run that opens with a heading takes that heading's space above it.

<div class="bx--prose-flow">
  <Prose><p>Add the package with your package manager:</p></Prose>
  <CodeSnippet code="npm i carbon-components-svelte" />
  <Prose><p>Then import the styles once, in your app's entry point.</p></Prose>
  <Prose><h3>Themes</h3><p>Every theme ships in the same stylesheet.</p></Prose>
</div>

## Carbon components inside

Prose doesn't style Carbon components or anything inside them, so components keep their own styles: a `Link` keeps its color, a `CodeSnippet` its background and padding, and `Text` its type style. That includes plain elements you pass into a component's slot, such as the link in this notification. Components still take part in the spacing between elements: copy after a block component, such as a `CodeSnippet`, gets more room. Copy after an inline one, such as a `Link`, gets the usual gap, and so does copy after `Text` in any tag: it counts as copy.

<Prose>
  <h3>Install</h3>
  <p>Add the package with your package manager:</p>
  <CodeSnippet code="npm i carbon-components-svelte" />
  <InlineNotification lowContrast hideCloseButton kind="info" title="Styles">
    <svelte:fragment slot="subtitleChildren">Import the CSS once, as the <a href="#carbon-components-inside">setup guide</a> shows.</svelte:fragment>
  </InlineNotification>
  <Text type="helper-text-01">Text keeps its own type style inside Prose.</Text>
  <p>Read the <Link href="#carbon-components-inside">theming guide</Link> next.</p>
</Prose>

## Rendered HTML

Prose styles markup it didn't render, such as HTML from a Markdown renderer or a CMS, set with `{@html}`. Only render HTML you trust or have sanitized.

<FileSource src="/framed/Prose/ProseRenderedHtml" />

## Wrapped sections

Children of a `section` or `article` inside Prose are spaced the same way as children of Prose itself, so you can wrap each section, for example to give it an `id` for a [TableOfContents](/components/TableOfContents).

<Prose>
  <section>
    <h3>Profile</h3>
    <p>Your name, photo, and email address appear wherever teammates mention or assign you.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>Security</h3>
    <p>Change your password, turn on two-factor authentication, and review the devices signed in to your account.</p>
  </section>
</Prose>

## Nested

A Prose inside another sets its own copy: each element takes its type from the nearest Prose, so a compact block inside a default page reads at 14px, and its lists keep their bullets. It has its own variant's gap above and below it.

<Prose>
  <p>Requests are limited per key. When you hit the limit, the API returns status 429 with a header that says how long to wait.</p>
  <Prose variant="compact">
    <p>Compact Prose nested in the page's Prose, for a note or a sidebar:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Retry after the number of seconds in the header.</li>
      <li>Batch requests to stay under the limit.</li>
    </ul>
  </Prose>
  <p>Limits reset every minute.</p>
</Prose>

## Markdown output

Prose styles what Markdown renderers produce for GitHub-flavored Markdown, so rendered pages need no components.

### Aligned table columns

Cells keep the alignment renderers set with the `align` attribute for `:---:` and `---:` columns.

<Prose>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Region</th><th align="center">Status</th><th align="right">Requests</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>us-east</td><td align="center">OK</td><td align="right">6,204,118</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ap-south</td><td align="center">Degraded</td><td align="right">2,109,872</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Prose>

### Heading anchors

Add an `a` with the `bx--prose__heading-anchor` class inside a heading to link to it. On devices that hover, it stays hidden until the heading is hovered or the link is focused. Give it an `aria-label` that names the section, such as "Link to Setup", since screen readers would otherwise read "#".

Elements with an `id` inside Prose take `scroll-margin-top` from the `--ccs-prose-scroll-margin` custom property. Set it to a sticky header's height so links to a heading land below the header.

<Prose>
  <h4 id="prose-anchor-setup">Setup <a class="bx--prose__heading-anchor" href="#prose-anchor-setup" aria-label="Link to Setup">#</a></h4>
  <p>Create a project in the console, then generate an API key.</p>
</Prose>

### Alerts

GitHub alerts (`> [!NOTE]`) render as a `blockquote` with `data-alert` set to `note`, `tip`, `important`, `warning`, or `caution`. They take InlineNotification's colors without its JavaScript. Code in an alert, inline or in a block, sits one layer up, so it stands out from the alert's surface. In dark themes, alerts take the layer's color and step up on a [Layer](/components/Layer) as other surfaces do. Set `data-alert-title` to show a title, in the reader's language.

<Prose>
  <blockquote data-alert="note" data-alert-title="Note"><p>Keys are scoped to one project.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="tip" data-alert-title="Tip"><p>Use one key per environment:</p><pre><code>npm run deploy -- --env staging</code></pre></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="important" data-alert-title="Important"><p>Regions can't be changed after you create a project.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="warning" data-alert-title="Warning"><p>Requests over the limit return <code>429</code>.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="caution" data-alert-title="Caution"><p>Revoking a key breaks every client that uses it.</p></blockquote>
</Prose>

### Task lists

Items with the `task-list-item` class, as GitHub and remark-gfm render task lists, show their checkbox in place of the bullet. Wrap each checkbox and its text in a `label`, so the checkbox has an accessible name.

<Prose>
  <ul class="contains-task-list">
    <li class="task-list-item"><label><input type="checkbox" disabled checked /> Create the project</label></li>
    <li class="task-list-item"><label><input type="checkbox" disabled /> Generate an API key</label></li>
  </ul>
</Prose>

### Highlighted code

Prose sets the padding, type, and surface of `pre`. For token colors, render `highlight` from `carbon-components-svelte/syntax` inside it, and load `syntax-tokens.css` and `syntax.css` as for [CodeSnippet](/components/CodeSnippet#syntax-highlighting). The colors are Carbon's syntax tokens, so code follows the theme.

<FileSource src="/framed/Prose/ProseHighlightedCode" />

Other highlighters work too: color their tokens with the `--cds-syntax-*` properties from `syntax-tokens.css`. For Shiki's CSS variables theme:

```css
.bx--prose {
  --shiki-foreground: var(--cds-text-primary);
  --shiki-background: var(--cds-layer);
  --shiki-token-comment: var(--cds-syntax-comment);
  --shiki-token-keyword: var(--cds-syntax-keyword);
  --shiki-token-string: var(--cds-syntax-string);
  --shiki-token-constant: var(--cds-syntax-number);
  --shiki-token-function: var(--cds-syntax-function);
  --shiki-token-punctuation: var(--cds-syntax-punctuation);
}
```

## Usage

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Write plain elements (`p`, `h2`, `ul`, `a`) inside Prose, not `Text` for each one. | Prose styles plain markup. `Text` and other Carbon components keep their own type styles, so they don't match the copy around them. |
| Use Prose for copy you didn't lay out element by element: rendered Markdown, CMS content, or long help text. | That's where styling each element with a component isn't possible or isn't worth it. For a few lines in a layout, `Text` gives finer control. |
| Use `compact` inside product UI and `default` on pages people read from top to bottom. | Product UI is set at 14px, so 16px copy inside a panel looks out of scale. |
| Set `maxWidth="none"` when the container already limits the width. | The line length cap would otherwise leave a gap beside the copy in a wide container. |
| Use `linkTone="muted"` and `kbd="muted"` where links and keys are dense, such as reference docs and shortcut lists. | Link-colored or boxed runs every few words pull the eye away from the copy. |
| Keep `tableWidth="fit"` for short tables, and use `"full"` for wide ones. | A two-column table stretched across the measure puts its columns far apart. |
| Set `--ccs-prose-scroll-margin` to a sticky header's height. | Links to headings and sections land below the header instead of under it. |
| Restyle an element with one class and its element, such as `p.lead`. | Prose's element rules weigh one class and one element, so a class alone ties or loses. |
| Put a Prose directly inside another Prose, or in a plain wrapper, rather than inside a Carbon component that sits in Prose. | Prose skips everything inside a Carbon component within it, and it can't tell a component's content from a Prose nested there, so Prose inside a Tile, Accordion, or Modal inside Prose renders unstyled. Telling them apart needs CSS `@scope`, which is newer than the browsers Carbon supports. |
